00:00:25.228 --> 00:00:29.410
Now it’s not really hard to see how heliozoa
got their name.

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:35.500
With their spheroid body and spiky rays, it
seems almost impossible to not pick a name

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:37.460
related to the sun.

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:41.440
So why not refer back to the Greek god of
the sun Helios?

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:46.470
Of course, heliozoa aren’t exactly riding
golden chariots across the sky, but they’ve

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:49.430
been similarly difficult to pin down.

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:54.710
And that’s partly because we’ve come to
understand that labeling things as heliozoa

00:00:54.710 --> 00:01:00.720
might not be as meaningful as we once thought
it was.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:05.940
In the 19th century, when heliozoa were first
described, the label was thought to apply

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:13.180
to just about any spiky, freshwater organisms
that looked like spiky, freshwater organisms.

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:18.440
The similarity in their appearance suggested
a proximity in their categorization, so it

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:21.080
made sense to group them together.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:25.580
That has of course changed in more recent
years as scientists have turned to studies

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:33.030
of organisms' DNA to revise those early classification
systems so they better match genetic relationships.

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:40.160
So today, “heliozoa” is less of a specific
label and more of a description, referring

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:46.350
to a set of organisms that are round with
stiff pseudopodia or axopodia, the more technical

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:48.010
term for those spikes.

00:01:48.010 --> 00:02:00.170
And they are not necessarily closely related
to one other, they just look like it.

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:05.110
If a heliozoan’s axopodia seem threatening
to you, that’s good.

00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:10.489
Those stiff spikes aren’t there as decoration,
they play an important role in the heliozoan’s

00:02:10.489 --> 00:02:15.400
hunt for food, whether that’s other protists
or even tiny animals.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:22.930
And you should probably think of them less
as spikes and more as sticky skewers.

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:28.129
After the heliozoan’s prey gets caught in
the axopodia, it is immediately immobilized

00:02:28.129 --> 00:02:30.540
and stuck to the adhesive surface.

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:37.040
The prey then gets drawn in towards the heliozoan
itself until it is close enough to be engulfed

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:40.550
into a vacuole for further digestion.

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:46.830
The central, driving life of the heliozoan
is in that central body, where food is consumed

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:51.870
and the important biological molecules that
keep the organism alive are made.

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:54.719
And what a fine, spherical body it is.

00:02:54.719 --> 00:03:00.809
But let’s be honest: it’s the axopodia
that makes a heliozoa so striking to look at.

00:03:00.809 --> 00:03:05.419
And for as long as we’ve been looking at
heliozoa, we have wanted to know more about

00:03:05.419 --> 00:03:09.090
how those axopodia work and how they’re
made.

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:12.120
After all, axopodia have quite a lot to do.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:15.800
They’re not simply stagnant spikes on a
heliozoan.

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:21.859
They’re an extension of the organism’s
cytoplasm, carrying the extrusomes that make

00:03:21.859 --> 00:03:23.939
their surface adhesive.

00:03:23.939 --> 00:03:28.840
And they contract when the organism needs
them to, like when they’re drawing in their prey.

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:34.309
It’s tempting to compare the axopodia to
having just a bunch of arms, but the comparison

00:03:34.309 --> 00:03:37.040
doesn’t do the axopodia justice.

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:42.510
After all, it’s not like we can retract
and extend our arms out of our bodies at will.

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:48.680
While axopodia are, in a sense, the cytoplasm
poking outwards, there needs to be something

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:52.480
underlying those extensions to give them shape
and structure.

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:57.730
And that “something” are hollow filaments
called microtubules.

00:03:57.730 --> 00:04:04.269
Made from a protein tubulin, you can think
of microtubules as building blocks for eukaryotic

00:04:04.269 --> 00:04:09.159
cells, though they’re shaped like rods,
not actual blocks.

00:04:09.159 --> 00:04:14.400
Microtubules show up in mitotic spindles,
cilia, flagella, and many other pieces of

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:16.070
cellular machinery.

00:04:16.070 --> 00:04:22.449
And how they’re arranged inside an organism—whether
they are unicellular or multicellular—impacts

00:04:22.449 --> 00:04:25.069
everything from shape to movement.

00:04:25.069 --> 00:04:28.040
And heliozoa’s axopodia are no different.

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:34.879
In the 1960s, scientists wanted to understand
how the microtubules arranged and rearranged

00:04:34.879 --> 00:04:40.819
themselves, and how this impacted the heliozoa’s
response to changes in the world around them.

00:04:40.819 --> 00:04:46.240
To get a much, much closer look than we can
do here, they used electron microscopy, which

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:51.460
revealed that the microtubules were arranged
parallel to one another.

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:56.270
And when they looked at the cross-section
of the axopodia, they found that the microtubules

00:04:56.270 --> 00:04:59.539
formed an interlocking double-coil formation.

00:04:59.539 --> 00:05:04.319
But scientists are rarely content with just
knowing the structure of a thing.

00:05:04.319 --> 00:05:09.400
They also wanted to know how that structure
responds to all sorts of stimuli.

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:15.430
In the second half of the 60s, Lewis G. Tilney
and an assortment of different collaborators

00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:22.480
published a multi-part series of papers titled
“Studies on microtubules in heliozoa.”

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:27.150
We think some selections from the titles convey
the goal of these studies….

00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:31.920
We have part 2: “The effect of low temperature
on these structures in the formation and maintenance

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:33.139
of the axopodia.”

00:05:33.139 --> 00:05:38.110
Then there’s the sequel in part 3: the “pressure
analysis of the role of these structures in

00:05:38.110 --> 00:05:42.750
the formation and maintenance of axopodia”
And yes, there’s a part 4, titled “The

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:47.099
effect of colchicine in the formation and
maintenance of the axopodia.”

00:05:47.099 --> 00:05:52.360
That is a lot of analysis on the formation
and maintenance of the axopodia.

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:57.729
And these experiments helped to not only see
what happens to heliozoa in changing conditions,

00:05:57.729 --> 00:06:02.539
they helped our understanding of the microtubules
inside them.

00:06:02.539 --> 00:06:08.110
For example, when the researchers lowered
the temperature of a heliozoan, they found

00:06:08.110 --> 00:06:13.789
that the organism’s axopodia shortened until
they disappeared, a result of the chemical

00:06:13.789 --> 00:06:20.199
connections holding the microtubules breaking
down and the structure depolymerizing.

00:06:20.199 --> 00:06:25.379
And it was only around 30 to 45 minutes after
the heliozoa were returned to room temperature

00:06:25.379 --> 00:06:30.949
that the microtubules reassembled and the
axopodia grew back to their normal length.

00:06:30.949 --> 00:06:35.810
This result, combined with other observations
they made with their other disruptions, helped

00:06:35.810 --> 00:06:41.310
to solidify the microtubule’s importance
in the growth and structure of the axopodia,

00:06:41.310 --> 00:06:46.500
and provided us with more insight into the
cold-sensitivity of microtubules.

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:51.210
These experiments were not the end of the
“poke and prod and disrupt the axopodia”

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:53.259
approach to heliozoan research.

00:06:53.259 --> 00:06:58.380
A later study would use what feels almost
a poetic method—or at least as poetic as

00:06:58.380 --> 00:07:02.949
you can get with a science experiment and
a microbe named after a sun god.

00:07:02.949 --> 00:07:08.259
The researchers subjected their heliozoan
to ultraviolet light, watching as the ends

00:07:08.259 --> 00:07:14.069
of the axopodia broke down and shortened,
or sometimes even broke off altogether.

00:07:14.069 --> 00:07:20.949
And yet, even then, when the ultraviolet light
had passed, the sun animalcule’s microtubules

00:07:20.949 --> 00:07:24.000
were ready to grow once more.

